From 9f397f42cb6d1c75e6c4e3d2929e942823b93058 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Iain Sandoe <iain@sandoe.co.uk>
Date: Tue, 26 Jul 2022 12:17:42 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 38/97] c++, tls init: Temporary support for cross-tu static
 initialization [PR106435].

This patch set has been reviewed and there are suggestions for improvements
or a revised version.  However, in the short-term this is a usable placeholder.

===

1/ Currently, cross-tu static initialisation is not supported for targets without
alias support.

This patch adds support by building a stub function in place of the alias for
such targets.  Although this is slightly less efficient, it is a matter of
correcting wrong code and, for targets supporting sibcalls, actually the
penalty is a single jump for optimised code.

Signed-off-by: Iain Sandoe <iain@sandoe.co.uk>

	PR c++/106435

gcc/c-family/ChangeLog:

	* c-opts.cc (c_common_post_options): Allow fextern-tls-init for targets
	without alias support.

gcc/cp/ChangeLog:

	* decl2.cc (get_tls_init_fn): Allow targets without alias support.
	(handle_tls_init): Emit aliases for single init functions where the
	target supporst this, otherwise emit a stub function that calls the
	main tls init function.

gcc/testsuite/ChangeLog:

	* g++.dg/cpp0x/pr106435-b.cc: New test.
	* g++.dg/cpp0x/pr106435.C: New test.
	* g++.dg/cpp0x/pr106435.h: New test.

2/ C++, TLS, Darwin: Work around Darwin weak ref handling.

In order to handle cross-TU dynamic initialization of thread local variables,
we generate wrapper functions that call to the handler for the variable init.

Those inits are guarded so that they only run once.

From the perspective of a TU referencing a TLS variable, there is no way to
determine if it requires a guarded dynamic init.  So we make a weak reference
to said init and test for it at runtime.

This is fine at runtime for Darwin, but fails at link time when we have a TLS
var which does NOT need a guarded dynamic init (because then there is no init
and Darwin's ld64 does not permit undefined weak references at link time).

This patch is a work-around;  In the TU that is referencing the var, we now
generate both the wrapper _and_ a weak definition of a dummy init function.

So, in the case that there _is_ a dynamic init, the weak copy will be over-
ridden by the linker.  In the case that we have a trivial static init the
weak defined dummy init will be used.

TODO: rework this code-gen path to remove the test for the function (it will
always be true) since the combination of static and dynamic linkers will now
be responsible for correct operation.
